- slug: ch10-ex2-asa-triangle.svg
  explanation: Explains constructing a triangle when one side and two angles are given (QR = 3.8 cm, ∠P = 60°, ∠Q = 90°). Features angle calculation (∠R = 30°) and intersecting rays QA and RB[cite: 1].

- slug: ch10-ex3-ambiguous-single.svg
  explanation: Demonstrates the ambiguous case where two sides and a non-included angle result in exactly one valid triangle (AB = 5.3 cm, BC = 4.8 cm, ∠A = 60°)[cite: 1].

- slug: ch10-ex4-ambiguous-double.svg
  explanation: Illustrates the ambiguous case where an arc intersects a ray at two distinct points (C and C'), forming two valid triangles (ABC and ABC' from sides 4.8 cm, 4.3 cm, and ∠B = 60°)[cite: 1].

- slug: ch10-ex5-ambiguous-impossible.svg
  explanation: Shows the impossible construction scenario where the arc radius is too short to intersect the angle ray (∠MLA = 30°, side 5.4 cm, opposite side 2.3 cm)[cite: 1].

